{
  "title": "Calorie architecture phase 5: move quantity parsing out of nutrition so calories are a lookup",
  "description": "Phase 5 of the design in doc/calorie-estimates.md (\"Architecture\"), approved 2026-10-11. ramekin-core/src/nutrition/mod.rs (about 1,180 lines, one file) parses quantities itself: a range and fraction grammar (quantity()), \"plus\" compounds (measurement_total), per-item weights (\"2 cans, 10 oz each\", per_item_grams), package weights from units and notes (package_grams, noted_package_unit), and \"3-inch piece\" sizes. \
That is parsing, not calories. \
Move it into ingredient parser normalization so a parsed line carries a numeric amount (or range) and a canonical unit, and nutrition becomes: grams = amount x (mass constant | grams_per_cup x volume factor | piece weight | package weight), kcal = grams x kcal/100g, plus the trace and frying rules. \
Coordinate with p3-ingredient-parser-rewrite, which proposes a tokenizer and grammar for the parser; doing this after that may be simpler. \
Pin behavior first with ramekin-core/tests/nutrition_tests.rs and the pipeline fixtures; run make pipeline and review data/ diffs. \
Also: a weight stated only in the note of a bare-count line is ignored (\"3-to-4-rib roast of beef\" with note \"approximately 8 to 10 pounds\", cream cheese with note \"8 ounce packages\"), so the line stays unknown; normalization should turn it into a weight measurement (found in PR review of p2-calorie-weights-in-catalog, which left those foods' piece estimates null rather than guess).",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 3,
  "type": "task",
